WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:12.459
Oliver's ailments were neither slight nor few. Besides his wounded arm, he suffered from a fever, which gave him aches and chills and hung on for many weeks. But at last he began to get better.

2
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:18.943
Sometimes he told Rose and Mrs. Maylie, in a few tearful words, how much he appreciated what they had done for him.

3
00:00:18.944 --> 00:00:22.110
He wished he could do something to show his gratitude.

4
00:00:22.111 --> 00:00:29.348
"Poor fellow," said Rose one day when he was thanking her. "You will have many opportunities to serve us if you want.

5
00:00:29.349 --> 00:00:37.015
We are soon going to the country. The quiet place, the pure air, and the beauties of spring will restore you in a few days.

6
00:00:37.016 --> 00:00:40.848
We will employ you in a hundred ways when you are strong enough to work."

7
00:00:40.849 --> 00:00:51.165
"Oh, dear lady," cried Oliver. "If only I could work for you now. If only I could give you pleasure by watering your flowers or running errands!"

8
00:00:51.166 --> 00:00:59.491
"You will make me happier than I can tell you," replied Rose. "I'm already happy that my good aunt rescued you from such a miserable life.

9
00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:06.983
But it makes me even happier to know that you're grateful. Do you understand me?" she asked, watching Oliver's thoughtful face.

10
00:01:06.984 --> 00:01:14.158
"Oh yes, miss, yes," Oliver replied eagerly. "I was just thinking that I am being ungrateful now."

11
00:01:14.159 --> 00:01:16.920
"To whom?" inquired Rose.

12
00:01:16.921 --> 00:01:25.230
"To the kind gentleman and his dear old housekeeper who took such good care of me before. I'm sure they would be pleased to know how happy I am."

13
00:01:25.231 --> 00:01:31.976
"I'm sure they would. In fact, Dr. Losberne has already promised that he will take you to see them soon."

14
00:01:31.977 --> 00:01:40.642
"He has?" Oliver's face brightened with pleasure. "I don't know what I'll do when I see their kind faces again."

15
00:01:40.643 --> 00:01:45.467
In a short time, Oliver was well enough to undertake the trip to Mr. Brownlow's house.

16
00:01:45.468 --> 00:01:50.285
One morning he and Dr. Losberne set out in a coach that belonged to Mrs. Maylie.

17
00:01:50.286 --> 00:01:56.380
Soon after passing through Chertsey, Oliver suddenly turned very pale and uttered a loud exclamation.

18
00:01:56.381 --> 00:02:06.102
"What's the matter?" cried the doctor, all in a bustle as usual. "Do you see anything? Hear anything? Feel anything? Eh?"

19
00:02:06.103 --> 00:02:11.690
"That, sir!" cried Oliver, pointing out the carriage window. "That house!"

20
00:02:11.691 --> 00:02:19.943
"Yes, what about it? Stop, coachman. Pull up here!" cried Dr. Losberne. "What about the house?"

21
00:02:19.944 --> 00:02:27.599
"The robber . . . Bill Sikes," whispered Oliver. "That's the house he took me to, where we met the other robber."

22
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.574
"It is? Let me out, coachman."

23
00:02:31.575 --> 00:02:40.567
But before the coachman could climb down, Dr. Losberne had rushed out of the coach. Running over to the deserted house, he began kicking at the door.

24
00:02:40.568 --> 00:02:49.154
"Hello? What's the matter?" A little hunchbacked man opened the door so suddenly that the doctor almost fell into the front hall.

25
00:02:49.155 --> 00:02:55.083
"Matter!" exclaimed the doctor, grabbing the man by the collar. "Robbery is the matter."

26
00:02:55.084 --> 00:02:58.908
"Murder will be the matter too, if you don't take your hands off me."

27
00:02:58.909 --> 00:03:08.130
"Where is that robber fellow?" Dr. Losberne demanded. "What's his name? Sikes, that's it. Where's Sikes, you thief?"

28
00:03:08.131 --> 00:03:15.392
The man twisted himself out of the doctor's grasp. He growled some horrid oaths and then retired into the house.

29
00:03:15.393 --> 00:03:20.281
But before the man could shut the door, the doctor had followed him in.

30
00:03:20.282 --> 00:03:30.194
Dr. Losberne went into the parlor and looked anxiously around. Nothing matched Oliver's description. Not the room. Not the furniture.

31
00:03:30.195 --> 00:03:36.622
He passed into another parlor, which again bore no resemblance whatsoever to Oliver's account of the house.

32
00:03:36.623 --> 00:03:39.852
"What do you want?" screamed the little man.

33
00:03:39.853 --> 00:03:46.447
"The boy must have made a mistake," Dr. Losberne muttered to himself. "Here, put that in your pocket!"

34
00:03:46.448 --> 00:03:51.138
With those words he flung some money at the man and hurried out to the carriage.

35
00:03:51.139 --> 00:03:56.447
The man followed him to the carriage door, uttering the wildest curses and exclamations.

36
00:03:56.448 --> 00:04:03.177
Spying Oliver, the man gave him a glance so fierce and vindictive that Oliver could not forget it for months afterward.

37
00:04:03.178 --> 00:04:13.169
The coachman resumed his seat and they were off. Looking back, Oliver and Dr. Losberne could see the little man stamping his feet and tearing his hair in rage.

38
00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:22.582
"I'm a fool," said Dr. Losberne after a long silence. "Even if it had been the right place and the right fellows had been there, what could I have done by myself?"

39
00:04:22.583 --> 00:04:27.741
The doctor sat brooding for a few minutes but soon returned to his usual good temper.

40
00:04:27.742 --> 00:04:34.415
Oliver's replies to his questions were as sincere as ever, so the doctor decided to continue to trust the boy.

41
00:04:34.416 --> 00:04:39.661
Because Oliver knew the name of Mr. Brownlow's street, they were able to drive straight there.

42
00:04:39.662 --> 00:04:45.058
When the coach turned into it, Oliver's heart was beating so hard that he could barely breathe.

43
00:04:45.059 --> 00:04:48.971
"Now, my boy, which house is it?" inquired Dr. Losberne.

44
00:04:48.972 --> 00:04:59.479
"That one!" replied Oliver, pointing eagerly out the coach window. "The white one. Oh, hurry. I'm so excited I could die."

45
00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:07.891
"Now, now." The good doctor patted him on the shoulder. "You'll see them soon, and they'll be overjoyed to find you safe and well."

46
00:05:07.892 --> 00:05:12.129
"Oh, I hope so. They were so very good to me."

47
00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:18.716
The coach stopped. No, that was the wrong house. It went on again and stopped.

48
00:05:18.717 --> 00:05:23.701
Oliver looked up at the windows with happy tears of expectation running down his face.

49
00:05:23.702 --> 00:05:27.708
Alas, the house was empty with a "For Rent" sign in the window.

50
00:05:27.709 --> 00:05:34.113
Dr. Losberne got out of the coach and took Oliver by the hand. They knocked on the door of the next house.

51
00:05:34.114 --> 00:05:38.771
The servant told them that Mr. Brownlow and his staff had left for the West Indies.

52
00:05:38.772 --> 00:05:44.779
Oliver sank feebly backward. Dr. Losberne guided him into the coach.

53
00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:48.581
"Head for home," Dr. Losberne directed the coachman.

54
00:05:48.582 --> 00:05:56.930
"But what about the bookseller?" pleaded Oliver. "I know the way there. Maybe he could tell us more about Mr. Brownlow."

55
00:05:56.931 --> 00:06:01.010
"My poor boy, this is disappointment enough for one day."

56
00:06:01.011 --> 00:06:09.327
Oliver was filled with sorrow. Many times during his illness, he had cheered himself with the thought of greeting Mr. Brownlow and Mrs. Bedwin again.

57
00:06:09.328 --> 00:06:16.248
He wanted to thank them. He had been buoyed up by the hope of explaining how he had been snatched away from them.

58
00:06:16.249 --> 00:06:22.414
Now they had gone far away, carrying with them the belief that he was an impostor and a robber.

59
00:06:22.415 --> 00:06:31.630
He might never have a chance to explain everything to them. Thinking about that was almost too much for Oliver to bear.

